.TH SDG 2
.SH NAME
sdg \- Styx over Noise-secured datagrams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sdg.m";
sdg := load Sdg Sdg->PATH;

init:  fn(): string;
Keys:  adt {
	key, server, psk: array of byte;
	ctrfile: string;
};
relay: fn(addr: string, keys: ref Keys, fd: ref Sys->FD): string;
ctrnext: fn(f: string, now: big): (big, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Sdg
carries 9P2000 one message per datagram, secured with
.BR Noise_IKpsk2_25519_ChaChaPoly_SHA256 ,
in place of a byte stream.
Each request is retransmitted under its own request id until it is
answered; the server keeps recent replies, so a retransmitted request is
answered again but never run twice.
A lost packet costs one retransmission (40 ms), not a stream stall.
The protocol is specified in the styxbrick repository's
.BR docs/STYX-DGRAM.md .
.PP
.B Init
loads the modules
.B sdg
needs and returns nil or an error.
.PP
.B Relay
dials
.I addr
(a dial string such as
.BR udp!host!5640 ),
performs the handshake with the
.I keys
\- our X25519 private key, the server's public key and the pre-shared
key, 32 bytes each \- and then relays 9P between
.I fd
and the session until
.I fd
is closed.
.I Keys.ctrfile
names the handshake counter's store (see
.B ctrnext
below).
It returns nil, or an error if the handshake failed.
Before anything is sent it refuses keys that are not 32 bytes each, a
server key that gives no shared secret with ours (a low-order point:
X25519's all-zero result), and a counter that cannot be committed; and
it drops a response whose ephemeral key gives no shared secret, as one
that does not authenticate.
.PP
.B Ctrnext
gives the next handshake counter for the store
.IR f :
at least
.I now
(nanoseconds since the epoch; the clock's, when
.B relay
asks), and more than any value it has given before, so it never repeats
or goes back across restarts and clock steps.
The store is two files,
.IB f .a
and
.IB f .b ,
each a line holding a value and a check of it; a new value is written
over the older, synced, and read back before it is returned, so a crash
or a short write spoils only the file being written, whose value was
never sent.
Callers take turns through
.IB f .lock ,
made exclusively and remove-on-close, so the exact owner's exit removes it.
The lock is never reclaimed merely because its timestamp is old: a paused
owner and a wall-clock step must not create two counter allocators.
A file
.I f
holding a bare value, as earlier versions kept it, is read as well.
It returns an error, and no value, when the store cannot be written or
read back, or holds nothing it can trust (no valid file, and a damaged
one): the server refuses a counter not newer than the last it accepted,
so a value is not guessed.
Typically
.I fd
is one end of a pipe whose other end is given to
.IR sys-bind (2)'s
.BR mount .
.B Relay
accepts only complete 9P request frames between 7 bytes and
.B MSIZE
and only authenticated replies whose inner length is exact, whose tag is the
pending request's, and whose type is that request's reply or
.BR Rerror .
A structural violation ends the session; authentication does not make an
ambiguous byte stream safe to pass to the mount driver.
.PP
Every request gets an answer: the server's reply; or, made by
.BR relay ,
the error
.B "timed out"
when its deadline
.RB ( DEADLINE ,
5 s) passes with no word from the server
.RB ( "outcome unknown: timed out"
for a write, which may or may not have been applied);
or
.B "link down"
when a request the server has acknowledged as waiting for data (a
blocking read) stops being acknowledged for
.B LIVE
(5 s); or
.B "reply gone"
.RB ( "outcome unknown: reply gone"
for a write) at once when the server says it will never run the request
(its reply has left the server's cache, or the request came too late);
a Tflush so answered gets its Rflush.
A blocking read so acknowledged has no deadline.
The session outlives outages; an idle one sends a keepalive every
.B IDLE
(15 s).
If requests wait for a minute with no authenticated word from the server
(it rebooted, was re-paired, or another client took over), its 9P state is
gone either way:
.B relay
then hangs up, so callers get an error at once and can mount again.
A flushed request is kept until its
.B Rflush
arrives, so a reply that comes first is still delivered, as 9P allows.
Under a handshake flood the server answers with a cookie, which
.B relay
uses at once.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/sdg.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR brick (1)
